# m2_full_data.py - Complete Module 2 topics data
M2_DATA = {'m2-t1': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why does processing 1,000,000 soil sensor readings in a standard Python list take seconds and drain battery, while a NumPy array finishes in milliseconds?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Python lists store pointers to scattered heap objects, incurring dynamic type checking and memory fragmentation overhead. A NumPy ndarray is stored as a contiguous block of homogeneous C-memory with fixed byte strides, leveraging hardware SIMD vectorization and cache lines for orders-of-magnitude faster computation with minimal power consumption.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> If a soil probe logs moisture at 10cm, 30cm, 60cm, and 100cm across 50 farm plots, how does NumPy indexing simplify extracting only the deep root-zone moisture (60cm and 100cm) across all plots?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> In NumPy, multidimensional array slicing enables `sensor_data[:, 2:]`—slicing all rows (plots) and selecting columns 2 and 3 (deep root zones) in zero-copy memory views without duplicating data.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Slicing & Benchmark Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live benchmark comparing pure Python lists against NumPy ndarrays over 1,000,000 sensor records, what performance difference was observed?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) NumPy executed vectorized mathematical operations over 50x faster than pure Python loops due to contiguous C-memory storage and SIMD CPU vectorization</li>\n                        <li>B) Python lists were twice as fast because they have dynamic type flexibility</li>\n                        <li>C) Both ran at identical speeds</li>\n                        <li>D) NumPy crashed because Python cannot allocate contiguous memory</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>NumPy executed over 50x faster due to contiguous memory allocation and vectorized SIMD instructions.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> NumPy bypasses Python\'s interpreter loop overhead by dispatching low-level vectorized C/Fortran routines directly to CPU registers, delivering massive speedups on agricultural sensor arrays.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Dynamic type checking in lists causes massive CPU pointer chasing and slows execution.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> NumPy releases the GIL and uses compiled C code, outperforming pure Python loops.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> NumPy directly allocates contiguous memory via C-level memory allocators.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Soil Vector Comparison Syndicate):</strong> During the Soil Sample Vector Audit, which NumPy boolean mask isolated all farm plots where soil moisture dropped below 20.0% OR soil pH became critically acidic (< 5.5)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) critical_plots = (moisture < 20.0) | (ph_vals < 5.5)</li>\n                        <li>B) critical_plots = moisture < 20.0 or ph_vals < 5.5</li>\n                        <li>C) critical_plots = moisture && ph_vals</li>\n                        <li>D) critical_plots = not (moisture >= 20.0)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>(moisture < 20.0) | (ph_vals < 5.5) using bitwise OR operator with element-wise parentheses.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In NumPy, logical combinations of boolean arrays require bitwise operators (`|` for OR, `&` for AND) with parentheses around each condition, because Python\'s logical `or`/`and` evaluate the truth value of the entire array rather than per-element.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Using standard \'or\' on a NumPy array throws a ValueError (\'truth value of an array is ambiguous\').</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Syntax \'&&\' is not a valid Python operator.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Negating moisture alone ignores the pH condition completely.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building an Array Normalization Script, what formula scaled raw nitrogen readings (ranging from 120 to 380 kg/ha) into a normalized [0, 1] range?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) norm_n = (nitrogen - np.min(nitrogen)) / (np.max(nitrogen) - np.min(nitrogen))</li>\n                        <li>B) norm_n = nitrogen / 1000</li>\n                        <li>C) norm_n = np.sqrt(nitrogen)</li>\n                        <li>D) norm_n = nitrogen - np.mean(nitrogen)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Min-max normalization: (x - min) / (max - min) mapped values strictly between 0.0 and 1.0.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Min-max normalization linearly shifts and scales agricultural feature arrays so the minimum maps to 0.0 and the maximum maps to 1.0, preventing large-scale features from dominating distance-based ML algorithms.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Dividing by an arbitrary 1000 does not guarantee a minimum of 0.0 or maximum of 1.0.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Square root compresses variance but does not bound values to [0, 1].</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Subtracting the mean centers data at zero (mean centering), but leaves values unscaled.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t2': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> How can a farm cooperative calculate the total nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium required across 20 distinct crop zones in a single matrix multiplication?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Represent the 20 crop zones as an Acreage Matrix `A` of shape (20, 1) and specific N-P-K nutrient dosage requirements as a Dosage Matrix `D` of shape (20, 3). Matrix multiplication `A.T @ D` computes the dot product of acres and nutrient rates simultaneously, yielding total kilograms of N, P, and K in a single microsecond operation.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does multiplying two agricultural matrices with mismatched inner dimensions (e.g., shape (10, 4) and (5, 3)) throw a ValueError?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Matrix multiplication `A @ B` requires that the number of columns in `A` matches the number of rows in `B`. In farm math, if `A` represents 10 plots each with 4 soil factors, `B` must have 4 input weights corresponding to those exact 4 soil factors. A shape mismatch means you are attempting to multiply 4 soil properties by 5 weights—a logical impossibility.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Matrix Allocation Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of matrix nutrient allocation, what NumPy operator performed linear algebraic matrix multiplication between the crop acreage vector and the nutrient dosage matrix?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) The `@` operator (or np.matmul())</li>\n                        <li>B) The `*` operator (element-wise multiplication)</li>\n                        <li>C) The `**` exponentiation operator</li>\n                        <li>D) The `//` floor division operator</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>The `@` operator (np.matmul()) performed true linear algebraic matrix dot products.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In Python 3.5+, the `@` operator computes matrix multiplication (inner dot products of rows and columns). The `*` operator performs element-wise Hadamard multiplication, which requires identical shapes and does not compute linear combinations.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> The `*` operator computes element-wise multiplication, not linear matrix dot products.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> The `**` operator raises matrix elements to exponential powers.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Floor division truncates division results and does not perform matrix math.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Multi-Factor Yield Transformation Studio):</strong> During the Matrix Transformation Studio, when multiplying a farm feature matrix of shape (50, 4) by a weight vector of shape (4, 1), what was the shape of the resulting predicted yield array?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) (50, 1)</li>\n                        <li>B) (4, 4)</li>\n                        <li>C) (50, 4)</li>\n                        <li>D) (4, 50)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>(50, 1) representing 50 individual plot yield predictions.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Multiplying an (m x k) matrix by a (k x n) matrix produces an (m x n) matrix. Here, (50 x 4) @ (4 x 1) collapses the inner dimension 4, producing a (50 x 1) vector of predicted yields for the 50 plots.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> A (4, 4) output ignores the 50 sample plots entirely.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> (50, 4) would imply features were not linearly combined.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> (4, 50) is the transposed orientation.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Farm Economics Matrix Engine, how did you compute total enterprise revenue given a sales quantity matrix Q and commodity price vector P?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) total_revenue = np.dot(Q, P)</li>\n                        <li>B) total_revenue = Q + P</li>\n                        <li>C) total_revenue = np.linalg.inv(Q)</li>\n                        <li>D) total_revenue = np.zeros_like(Q)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>np.dot(Q, P) computed the linear dot product summing quantity times price across commodities.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Computing revenue across multi-crop enterprises is a classic dot product: multiplying volumes sold by their corresponding market prices and summing the products across categories in a single vectorized step.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Adding quantities to prices is mathematically meaningless and produces dimensionality errors.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Matrix inversion is an algebraic solve tool, not a revenue summation.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Zeros_like creates an empty array of zeros.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t3': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> How does NumPy\'s \'broadcasting\' allow an agronomist to apply an 8% sensor temperature calibration offset to a matrix of 100,000 readings across 50 depths without making 50 duplicate copies of the offset?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Broadcasting automatically stretches compatible dimensions in virtual memory without allocating extra RAM. When adding a 1D calibration vector of shape (50,) to a 2D sensor matrix of shape (100000, 50), NumPy virtually expands the vector across all 100,000 rows, performing element-wise addition with zero memory duplication.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does attempting to add an array of shape (50, 3) to an array of shape (50, 2) throw a \'broadcast dimensions could not be aligned\' error?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> NumPy\'s broadcasting rule requires that trailing dimensions must either be equal or one of them must be 1. Comparing (50, 3) and (50, 2), trailing dimensions 3 and 2 are neither equal nor is either 1. NumPy cannot align 3 soil nutrients with 2 moisture sensors, rejecting the ambiguous operation.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Aerial Spray Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live aerial-spray simulation, how did broadcasting apply 3 distinct chemical nozzle dosage adjustments to a 10,000-cell flight GPS coordinate matrix?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) NumPy automatically broadcast the (3,) parameter vector across the (10000, 3) flight grid without replicating data in memory</li>\n                        <li>B) By running three nested while loops in Python</li>\n                        <li>C) By manually duplicating the 3 parameters 10,000 times into a new CSV file</li>\n                        <li>D) By deleting all flight data rows</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Broadcasting virtually stretched the (3,) vector across the (10000, 3) matrix without memory overhead.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> NumPy broadcasting aligns trailing dimensions: a 1D vector of shape (3,) is compatible with an array of shape (10000, 3) because the trailing dimension 3 matches, enabling instant zero-copy vectorized arithmetic.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Python while loops discard NumPy\'s C-level vectorization benefits.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Manual duplication wastes megabytes of RAM and slows computation.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Deleting flight data destroys navigation records.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Sensor Normalization Studio):</strong> During the Multi-Zone Sensor Normalization Studio, what dimension manipulation was required before subtracting a 1D plot baseline mean array of shape (50,) from a 2D matrix of shape (50, 24)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Reshaping the baseline array to (50, 1) using newaxis or .reshape(50, 1) so broadcasting aligns along columns</li>\n                        <li>B) Transposing the 24-hour matrix into a 1D array</li>\n                        <li>C) Flattening the entire matrix to zero</li>\n                        <li>D) Multiplying the array by 24</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Reshaped baseline from (50,) to (50, 1) so NumPy broadcasted across the 24 hourly columns.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> A 1D array of shape (50,) aligns with trailing dimensions (mismatching 24). Reshaping it to (50, 1) introduces a singleton column dimension, allowing NumPy to broadcast that column 24 times across the time axis.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Transposing into 1D loses the plot-by-hour matrix structure.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Flattening to zero deletes all baseline measurements.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying by 24 scales values without fixing dimension alignment.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Vectorized Sensor Calibration Script, which code cleanly broadcast a temperature correction scalar (+1.25°C) across all elements of an entire multi-depth array?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) calibrated_readings = raw_readings + 1.25</li>\n                        <li>B) calibrated_readings = [x + 1.25 for x in raw_readings]</li>\n                        <li>C) calibrated_readings = raw_readings.append(1.25)</li>\n                        <li>D) calibrated_readings = np.concatenate([raw_readings, 1.25])</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>raw_readings + 1.25 broadcasted the scalar across every element in the multidimensional array.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Scalars broadcast universally to any array shape in NumPy, applying the mathematical offset to every element without loops or reshape boilerplate.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> List comprehensions on multidimensional arrays yield nested list artifacts and slow down execution.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> NumPy arrays do not have an .append() method; the function is np.append().</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Concatenation changes array shape rather than adding a numerical offset.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t4': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> When analyzing crop yield across 100 farm plots, why does reporting only the mean yield give a dangerously misleading picture if 5 plots suffered complete pest devastation while 5 had record bumper crops?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> The arithmetic mean blends extremes together, concealing high yield volatility and risk. A farm with a mean of 50 quintals/ha could have all plots producing 50, or half producing 100 and half producing 0. Statistical dispersion metrics—variance, standard deviation, and interquartile range—reveal operational risk that average figures conceal.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why is the Pearson correlation coefficient between fertilizer application and crop yield incapable of proving that fertilizer caused the yield increase?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Correlation measures linear association, not biological causation! High fertilizer usage may correlate strongly with high yield, but both might be driven by a confounding variable: progressive farmers who can afford fertilizer also invest in superior drip irrigation and certified hybrid seeds.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Correlation Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live correlation demonstration on real farm telemetry, what did an r = -0.82 Pearson correlation between soil moisture deficit and vegetative canopy greenness indicate?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) A strong negative linear relationship: as soil moisture deficit increased (drier soil), canopy greenness significantly decreased</li>\n                        <li>B) That soil moisture deficit causes fungal leaf blight</li>\n                        <li>C) That the sensor wires were connected backwards</li>\n                        <li>D) That canopy greenness and soil moisture are completely independent</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>A strong negative linear correlation between soil moisture deficit and canopy greenness.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> A Pearson correlation r near -1.0 indicates a strong inverse relationship: as moisture deficit climbs (drought worsens), plant chlorophyll and vegetative vigor decline proportionally.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Correlation does not identify specific fungal biological pathogens.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Negative correlation is a natural physical relationship, not an electrical wiring error.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Independence would be indicated by a correlation coefficient near r = 0.0.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Multi-Factor Yield Correlation Studio):</strong> During the Multi-Factor Yield Studio, how did teams prevent extreme outlier yields (caused by a combine harvester scale calibration glitch of 999 quintals) from corrupting the baseline analysis?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By computing the median and Interquartile Range (IQR) alongside mean and standard deviation, and winsorizing values exceeding Q3 + 1.5*IQR</li>\n                        <li>B) By deleting all rows where yield was higher than 10 quintals</li>\n                        <li>C) By multiplying all yields by zero</li>\n                        <li>D) By replacing the entire column with random numbers</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Used robust non-parametric metrics (median, IQR) and trimmed/winsorized values exceeding 1.5*IQR bounds.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Non-parametric statistics (median and IQR) are robust against hardware anomalies, preventing rogue extreme sensor spikes from distorting baseline agricultural averages.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Deleting values above 10 quintals discards genuine high-yielding crop records.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying by zero erases the entire experimental dataset.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Random numbers destroy all empirical evidence.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Farm Correlation Profiler, which NumPy function computed the pairwise correlation matrix across soil pH, organic carbon, and grain yield simultaneously?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) np.corrcoef(farm_metrics, rowvar=False)</li>\n                        <li>B) np.std(farm_metrics)</li>\n                        <li>C) np.linalg.det(farm_metrics)</li>\n                        <li>D) np.unique(farm_metrics)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>np.corrcoef(farm_metrics, rowvar=False) computed the standardized pairwise correlation matrix.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> The `np.corrcoef()` function computes normalized covariance matrices (Pearson correlation coefficients) between all column variables when `rowvar=False`, returning a symmetric matrix of correlation values.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> np.std() computes standard deviation, not pairwise correlation.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> np.linalg.det() computes matrix determinants.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> np.unique() extracts unique elements.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t5': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is a Pandas Series with a DateTimeIndex vastly superior to a simple Python list or NumPy array for tracking daily rainfall at an agricultural research station?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> A pure array has only integer positions (0, 1, 2...). A Pandas Series with a DateTimeIndex enables calendar-aware intelligence: slicing by date ranges (`rainfall[\'2025-06\':\'2025-09\']`), resampling to monthly or seasonal aggregates (`rainfall.resample(\'M\').sum()`), and automatically aligning rainfall on exact dates with irrigation logs.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What happens if you add two Pandas Series with different, unaligned index labels (e.g., Series A indexed by plot IDs 1, 2, 3 and Series B indexed by 2, 3, 4)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Pandas performs automatic index alignment! It matches elements by index label, not numerical position. For plots 2 and 3, it adds values correctly. For plot 1 (missing in B) and plot 4 (missing in A), Pandas safely inserts `NaN` (Not a Number) to prevent silent data misalignment that occurs in pure Python lists.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Series Indexing Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, how did a Pandas Series with a DateTimeIndex simplify calculating total Kharif season rainfall from daily weather records?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By slicing directly with calendar strings: rainfall_series[\'2025-06-01\':\'2025-10-31\'].sum()</li>\n                        <li>B) By manually counting days on a physical calendar and writing a 120-line loop</li>\n                        <li>C) By converting all dates into floating-point numbers</li>\n                        <li>D) By deleting all non-monsoon months from the hard drive</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Label-based date slicing: series[\'2025-06-01\':\'2025-10-31\'].sum() computed seasonal precipitation.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Pandas DateTimeIndex supports partial string date slicing, enabling intuitive extraction and summation of agricultural seasons without manual calendar math.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Manual date loops are error-prone and fail to account for leap years or irregular sampling intervals.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Floating-point date representations lose timezone and calendar semantics.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Deleting months destroys year-round climatic history.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Rainfall Series Triage Syndicate):</strong> During the Village Rainfall Triage Syndicate, what Pandas method smoothed day-to-day rainfall noise to identify prolonged 14-day dry spells?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) rainfall_series.rolling(window=14).mean()</li>\n                        <li>B) rainfall_series.drop_duplicates()</li>\n                        <li>C) rainfall_series.sort_values(ascending=False)</li>\n                        <li>D) rainfall_series.tail(14)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>rolling(window=14).mean() computed a 14-day moving average to identify chronic dry spells.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Rolling window calculations compute moving statistics across sequential time windows, smoothing high-frequency daily fluctuations to highlight underlying drought trends.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> drop_duplicates() removes identical rainfall values, destroying the chronological timeline.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> sort_values() breaks the temporal order of dates.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> tail(14) merely previews the final 14 days of the series.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a 12-Month Agro-Climatic Series, how did you verify that missing sensor days were populated with forward-filled weather observations?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) series.ffill() (or series.fillna(method=\'ffill\'))</li>\n                        <li>B) series.dropna()</li>\n                        <li>C) series.clear()</li>\n                        <li>D) series.reset_index(drop=True)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>series.ffill() forward-filled missing observation gaps with the most recent valid measurement.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Forward-filling (`ffill()`) propagates the last observed valid measurement forward to fill missing temporal gaps, a common meteorological imputation practice for short sensor dropouts.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> dropna() deletes missing dates, creating temporal discontinuities in the time series.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> clear() empties the entire data structure.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> reset_index() discards the DateTimeIndex.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t6': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is an agricultural DataFrame considered a 2D relational table on steroids compared to an Excel spreadsheet?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Excel spreadsheets are manual, unversioned, prone to silent copy-paste formulas, and collapse at over 1,000,000 rows. A Pandas DataFrame is programmable, vectorized, handles millions of rows in memory, enforces strict schema types (dates, categories, floats), and integrates seamlessly with SQL databases and ML pipelines via automated Python scripts.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> When adding a calculated column to a DataFrame (e.g., `df[\'nitrogen_deficit\'] = df[\'target_n\'] - df[\'actual_n\']`), why must beginner programmers never use a `for index, row in df.iterrows():` loop?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Iterrows() converts each row into a new Pandas Series object inside an interpreted Python loop, destroying C-level optimizations and running 500x slower. Pandas column operations are vectorized: subtracting two columns directly performs compiled C-level SIMD operations across the entire column simultaneously in microseconds.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Multi-Column Engineering Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, what vectorized statement computed gross plot revenue from \'yield_quintal\' and \'price_per_quintal\' across 20,000 farm plots?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df[\'gross_revenue\'] = df[\'yield_quintal\'] * df[\'price_per_quintal\']</li>\n                        <li>B) A for loop using df.iterrows() manually multiplying each row and appending to a list</li>\n                        <li>C) df[\'gross_revenue\'] = df.sum()</li>\n                        <li>D) df.to_excel()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Vectorized column multiplication: df[\'gross_revenue\'] = df[\'yield_quintal\'] * df[\'price_per_quintal\'].</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Direct vectorized column operations leverage NumPy underlying arrays, executing in compiled C at lightning speed without slow Python loop overhead.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Iterrows loops run up to 500x slower and represent an anti-pattern in Pandas.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> df.sum() aggregates all columns together into a single scalar sum.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> to_excel() exports data to disk without computing revenue.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Cooperative Production Ledger Studio):</strong> During the Cooperative Ledger Studio, which DataFrame operation merged plot-level soil sensor readings with a separate member farmer demographic table based on a shared \'plot_id\' key?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) pd.merge(sensor_df, farmer_df, on=\'plot_id\', how=\'inner\')</li>\n                        <li>B) pd.concat([sensor_df, farmer_df], axis=0)</li>\n                        <li>C) sensor_df + farmer_df</li>\n                        <li>D) sensor_df.sort_index()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>pd.merge(sensor_df, farmer_df, on=\'plot_id\', how=\'inner\') executed a relational database join.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Relational database joins in Pandas are executed using `pd.merge()`, matching records between independent tables on a designated foreign key (`plot_id`).</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> concat along axis 0 stacks rows vertically rather than joining columns on a shared key.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Adding dataframes attempts arithmetic addition, not relational joining.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> sort_index() sorts index labels without merging tables.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a 10-Plot Agricultural Management DataFrame, how did you drop an obsolete legacy column \'old_sensor_id\' permanently?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df.drop(columns=[\'old_sensor_id\'], inplace=True)</li>\n                        <li>B) df.delete(\'old_sensor_id\')</li>\n                        <li>C) df[\'old_sensor_id\'] = None</li>\n                        <li>D) df.pop() without arguments</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df.drop(columns=[\'old_sensor_id\'], inplace=True) removed the column from memory.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> The `.drop(columns=[...])` method deletes designated columns from a DataFrame, modifying it in place when `inplace=True` or returning a new DataFrame.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> DataFrames do not have a `.delete()` method.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Setting to None leaves the column in the DataFrame filled with nulls.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> pop() requires the exact column name as an argument.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t7': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Before training a machine learning model on farm data, why should an Agri-Data Scientist always inspect `df.describe()` and `df.info()` rather than jumping straight into model fitting?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> `df.info()` reveals missing data counts and incorrect column data types (e.g., numbers stored as strings). `df.describe()` reveals statistical anomalies: minimum soil pH of -999 (a sensor disconnect error code), maximum soil moisture of 1000%, or extreme skewness that would corrupt gradient descent.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does a visual histogram of crop yield frequently show a bimodal (two-peaked) distribution rather than a classic bell-shaped Gaussian normal distribution?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Agricultural data reflects physical realities! A bimodal yield distribution usually signifies two distinct sub-populations in the dataset: e.g., irrigated plots producing high yields (peak 2) versus rainfed plots suffering drought stress (peak 1), or two distinct crop varieties combined in the same dataset.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Farm-Health Dashboard Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live farm-health profiling demo, what critical hardware anomaly was immediately exposed by running df[\'soil_temp\'].describe()?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) A minimum value of -999.0°C and maximum of 999.0°C, indicating uncleaned error codes transmitted by disconnected ADC sensor probes</li>\n                        <li>B) A perfectly Gaussian normal distribution</li>\n                        <li>C) The exact date of next year\'s harvest</li>\n                        <li>D) The farmer\'s bank account balance</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Min/max values of ±999.0°C revealed unhandled hardware error codes in the telemetry.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `df.describe()` immediately flags out-of-bounds extremes. Sensor hardware frequently transmits sentinel values like -999 or 9999 when detached or uncalibrated, which must be filtered before modeling.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Extreme sentinel values break normal Gaussian symmetry.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Statistical summaries describe historical data, not future calendar dates.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Soil temperature telemetry does not contain banking data.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Outlier Investigation Studio):</strong> During the Outlier Investigation Studio, how did teams determine whether a yield reading of 120 quintals/ha was a recording error or a genuine biological breakthrough?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By cross-referencing fertilizer application, drip fertigation logs, hybrid seed variety, and peer farmer yields from adjacent plots on the same date</li>\n                        <li>B) By immediately deleting the record because it was higher than the mean</li>\n                        <li>C) By flipping a coin</li>\n                        <li>D) By asking an LLM without providing the farm data</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Cross-referenced agronomic co-factors (inputs, variety, irrigation, neighbors) to validate biological plausibility.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Outliers are not always errors; in agriculture, they may represent elite hybrid performance or high management efficiency. True validation requires cross-referencing input logs and environmental co-factors.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Blindly deleting high values discards potential biological breakthroughs and distorts yield potential.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Coin flipping is unscientific and arbitrary.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Generic LLMs cannot verify empirical physical field trial records without data.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Farm Dataset Explorer, which code generated a multi-panel visual overview of yield distribution and soil pH?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df[[\'yield\', \'soil_ph\']].hist(bins=20, figsize=(10, 4)) followed by plt.show()</li>\n                        <li>B) df.print()</li>\n                        <li>C) plt.close(\'all\')</li>\n                        <li>D) df.values.sum()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df[[\'yield\', \'soil_ph\']].hist(bins=20) visualized frequency distributions for both variables.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Pandas integrates directly with Matplotlib: calling `.hist()` on selected DataFrame columns produces clean, multi-subplot histograms revealing skewness, bimodality, and outliers.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> DataFrames do not have a `.print()` method.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> plt.close() closes figures without rendering or displaying plots.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Summing all values produces an uninterpretable single number.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t8': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> What is the crucial difference between `.loc[]` and `.iloc[]` in Pandas when filtering agricultural plot records?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> `.loc[]` is label-based: `df.loc[\'Plot_102\', \'soil_ph\']` accesses data by explicit index names and column header strings. `.iloc[]` is integer-position based: `df.iloc[0, 3]` accesses data by raw zero-indexed memory position. Confusing the two causes catastrophic errors if plot IDs happen to be numbers (e.g., plot ID 100 located at row index 0).</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does filtering an agricultural DataFrame using standard chained indexing `df[df[\'crop\'] == \'Cotton\'][\'yield\'] = 50` trigger Pandas\' infamous `SettingWithCopyWarning`?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Chained indexing creates ambiguity: Python first evaluates `df[df[\'crop\'] == \'Cotton\']`, which might return a temporary copy rather than a direct reference (view) into the original DataFrame. Modifying `[\'yield\']` on that copy means the original DataFrame is silently unchanged! The safe, explicit syntax is `.loc`: `df.loc[df[\'crop\'] == \'Cotton\', \'yield\'] = 50`.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Multi-Condition Query Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, what Pandas `.loc` expression filtered all plots growing \'Wheat\' with soil pH < 6.0 while updating their \'lime_recommendation\' column to True?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df.loc[(df[\'crop\'] == \'Wheat\') & (df[\'soil_ph\'] < 6.0), \'lime_recommendation\'] = True</li>\n                        <li>B) df[df[\'crop\'] == \'Wheat\'][\'soil_ph\'] = True</li>\n                        <li>C) if df[\'crop\'] == \'Wheat\': df[\'lime\'] = True</li>\n                        <li>D) df.query(\'crop == Wheat and soil_ph < 6.0\').delete()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df.loc[(condition1) & (condition2), \'col\'] = True cleanly selected and mutated data without SettingWithCopyWarning.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Using `.loc[row_condition, target_column]` is the gold-standard Pandas idiom for conditional mutation, ensuring direct in-place modification of the original DataFrame.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Chained indexing triggers SettingWithCopyWarning and fails to update the original DataFrame.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Python\'s `if` statement cannot evaluate an entire Series truth value.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Querying and calling .delete() is invalid syntax.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Farm Subsidy Eligibility Studio):</strong> During the Subsidy Eligibility Screening Studio, which query operator correctly selected farmers who cultivated either \'Ragi\' OR \'Maize\' with landholding <= 2.0 hectares?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df[df[\'crop\'].isin([\'Ragi\', \'Maize\']) & (df[\'land_hectares\'] <= 2.0)]</li>\n                        <li>B) df[df[\'crop\'] == \'Ragi\' or df[\'crop\'] == \'Maize\']</li>\n                        <li>C) df.filter(items=[\'Ragi\', \'Maize\'])</li>\n                        <li>D) df[\'land_hectares\'].min()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df[\'crop\'].isin([\'Ragi\', \'Maize\']) combined with landholding <= 2.0 via bitwise & operator.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> The `.isin()` method provides clean, readable vectorized membership testing, which combines cleanly with numerical inequalities using the bitwise `&` operator.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Using Python\'s logical \'or\' throws a ValueError on Series comparisons.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> df.filter(items=...) filters column headers, not row values.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> min() returns a scalar number, not a filtered subset of farmers.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Farm Data Query Engine, what method selected rows based on a complex string query without verbose brackets?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df.query("soil_moisture < 25 and ambient_temp > 35")</li>\n                        <li>B) df.select("where moisture < 25")</li>\n                        <li>C) df.where_string("all")</li>\n                        <li>D) df.eval_string()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df.query("soil_moisture < 25 and ambient_temp > 35") provided clean, readable string-based query syntax.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Pandas `.query()` evaluates string expressions using numexpr under the hood, offering clean, uncluttered syntax for complex multi-variable filtering.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> DataFrames do not have a `.select()` method for SQL string clauses.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> `.where_string()` is a fictitious method.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> `.eval_string()` does not exist in Pandas.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t9': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> When an IoT soil moisture sensor goes offline for 3 days during a rainstorm, why is simply deleting those missing rows (`df.dropna()`) an agronomic disaster?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Deleting missing rows during a storm creates catastrophic selection bias! The missing records coincided with peak rainfall and soil saturation. Deleting them artificially removes the wettest days from the historical record, biasing long-term climatic averages toward dry conditions and breaking chronological time continuity required for hydrological modeling.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why should you never fill missing soil nitrogen values with the column mean (`df.fillna(df.mean())`) across a mixed farm dataset of legumes and cereals?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Legumes (like soybean or groundnut) fix atmospheric nitrogen, exhibiting vastly higher soil nitrogen levels than cereals (like maize or wheat). Imputing the global dataset mean overestimates nitrogen on cereal plots (risking fertilizer under-application) while underestimating nitrogen on legume plots. Imputation must be grouped by crop type: `df.groupby(\'crop\')[\'nitrogen\'].transform(\'median\')`.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Sensor Gap Rescue Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live telemetry demonstration, what imputation strategy accurately restored missing 15-minute temperature readings during a 2-hour sensor battery replacement?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Time-weighted linear interpolation: df[\'temperature\'].interpolate(method=\'time\')</li>\n                        <li>B) Filling all missing values with 0.0°C</li>\n                        <li>C) Dropping all preceding and succeeding rows</li>\n                        <li>D) Filling with the maximum temperature of the year</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Time-weighted linear interpolation: .interpolate(method=\'time\') smoothed between known bounds.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> For short temporal gaps in continuous physical environmental data, time-weighted interpolation estimates intermediate temperatures based on surrounding boundary measurements, preserving diurnal trends.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Imputing 0.0°C introduces artificial freezing temperatures and triggers false frost alarms.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Dropping surrounding valid rows destroys real measurements.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Filling with annual maximum creates false heatwave anomalies.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Sensor Failure Triage Studio):</strong> During the Sensor Failure Triage Studio, how did teams handle an experimental plot where an entire sensor node was destroyed by a wild boar and had 95% missing data?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Dropping the plot column entirely because imputing 95% of data creates artificial hallucinations with no empirical foundation</li>\n                        <li>B) Imputing all 95% missing values with random numbers</li>\n                        <li>C) Duplicating data from a farm 500 kilometers away</li>\n                        <li>D) Setting all values to 100</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Dropped the severely corrupted plot column because 95% missingness cannot be reliably imputed.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Data engineering best practices dictate that features or entities with >70–80% missingness should be pruned; synthetic imputation across 95% of records injects severe model hallucinations without empirical signal.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Imputing 95% random data corrupts machine learning models with pure noise.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Weather and soil 500 km away have zero local micro-climatic relevance.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Hardcoding 100 introduces extreme bias.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Missing Data Cleaning Pipeline, how did you verify the exact count and percentage of missing values per column?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) missing_summary = df.isnull().sum() / len(df) * 100</li>\n                        <li>B) missing_summary = df.count() * 100</li>\n                        <li>C) missing_summary = df.shape[0]</li>\n                        <li>D) missing_summary = df.empty</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df.isnull().sum() / len(df) * 100 computed the precise missing data percentage per column.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Chaining `.isnull().sum()` calculates the total count of nulls per column, which dividing by `len(df)` converts into percentage of missingness for triage.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> `.count()` returns the number of non-null values, not missing values.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> `.shape[0]` returns the total row count.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> `.empty` returns a single boolean indicating if the DataFrame has zero rows.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t10': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> How does Pandas\' Split-Apply-Combine paradigm mirror how a district agricultural commissioner analyzes harvest productivity across 10 taluks (sub-districts)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> A commissioner does not examine 50,000 individual farm rows. They \'Split\' the dataset into 10 separate taluk groups, \'Apply\' aggregate calculations to each group (mean yield, total fertilizer consumed, drought-affected acreage), and \'Combine\' the results into a compact 10-row district executive dashboard using `df.groupby(\'taluk\').agg({\'yield\': \'mean\', \'fertilizer\': \'sum\'})`.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does calculating the average yield across an entire state without grouping by crop type commit Simpson\'s Paradox?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Simpson\'s Paradox occurs when an apparent trend reverses or disappears when groups are aggregated! For example, high-value sugarcane yields 80 tons/ha while rainfed millets yield 2 tons/ha. If irrigated districts grow sugarcane and dry districts grow millet, an aggregated state-wide average gives meaningless numbers that reflect crop mix rather than actual farming efficiency.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Harvest Intelligence Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, what Pandas `groupby().agg()` statement computed both the mean yield and the maximum recorded soil moisture grouped by \'district\'?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df.groupby(\'district\').agg({\'yield\': \'mean\', \'moisture\': \'max\'})</li>\n                        <li>B) df.groupby(\'district\').sum()</li>\n                        <li>C) df.aggregate([\'mean\', \'max\'])</li>\n                        <li>D) df.pivot_table(index=\'district\')</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df.groupby(\'district\').agg({\'yield\': \'mean\', \'moisture\': \'max\'}) computed custom metrics per group.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Passing a dictionary mapping column names to aggregation functions inside `.agg()` provides fine-grained control over group-wise summaries across multiple variables.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> `.sum()` applies summation to all columns, which is meaningless for soil moisture percentages.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Calling `.aggregate()` without groupby computes statewide totals without district grouping.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Basic pivot_table without aggfunc defaults to mean across all columns.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (District Harvest Intelligence Studio):</strong> During the District Harvest Studio, how did teams restructure harvest data to create a cross-tabulated matrix showing average crop yield with \'district\' as rows and \'crop_type\' as columns?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df.pivot_table(index=\'district\', columns=\'crop_type\', values=\'yield\', aggfunc=\'mean\')</li>\n                        <li>B) df.melt(id_vars=\'district\')</li>\n                        <li>C) df.unstack().dropna()</li>\n                        <li>D) df.transpose()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df.pivot_table(index=\'district\', columns=\'crop_type\', values=\'yield\', aggfunc=\'mean\') created a 2D cross-tabulation.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Pandas `.pivot_table()` reshapes long-format telemetry into a compact 2D matrix, enabling comparative evaluation of crop yields across districts and crop types.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> `.melt()` unpivots wide data into long format, the exact opposite of pivot_table.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> unstack() requires hierarchical MultiIndex series.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> `.transpose()` swaps rows and columns without aggregating values.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Farm Aggregation Report, how did you filter to retain only districts that had more than 100 reporting farm plots?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) df.groupby(\'district\').filter(lambda x: len(x) > 100)</li>\n                        <li>B) df[df[\'district\'] > 100]</li>\n                        <li>C) df.drop_duplicates()</li>\n                        <li>D) df.head(100)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>df.groupby(\'district\').filter(lambda x: len(x) > 100) filtered groups based on group size.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> The `.filter()` method on a GroupBy object applies a boolean predicate function to each sub-dataframe, discarding groups that fail the condition (e.g., sample size < 100).</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Comparing string district names with integer 100 throws a TypeError.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> drop_duplicates() removes repeated rows without group size filtering.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> head(100) returns the first 100 rows of the dataframe.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t11': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is plotting daily rainfall (bar chart in mm) and daily ambient temperature (line chart in °C) on the same single Y-axis visually misleading, and how do dual-axis subplots solve this?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Rainfall ranges from 0 to 100mm, while temperature ranges from 15°C to 40°C. Plotting both on one Y-axis compresses the temperature curve into an unreadable flat line or distorts rainfall bars. Using Matplotlib\'s `ax.twinx()` creates a dual-axis chart with two independent scales (left for rain mm, right for temp °C), allowing farmers to correlate rain events directly with temperature drops.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why should an AgriTech dashboard for smallholder farmers favor high-contrast simple charts with clear annotations over complex, cluttered multi-dimensional plots?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Farmers review advisories in bright sunlight on low-cost smartphones while working in dusty fields. Cluttered 3D graphs with small legends are unreadable in real-world conditions. High-contrast 2D line and bar charts with large threshold danger zones (e.g., a red dotted line at 38°C for heat stress) deliver immediate, life-saving clarity.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Dual-Axis Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, which Matplotlib method constructed a secondary Y-axis sharing the same X-axis timeline to overlay rainfall bars with temperature lines?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) ax2 = ax1.twinx()</li>\n                        <li>B) plt.copy_axis()</li>\n                        <li>C) ax2 = ax1.twiny()</li>\n                        <li>D) plt.subplot(2, 2, 2)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>ax1.twinx() created an independent twin Y-axis sharing the common time X-axis.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Matplotlib\'s `.twinx()` creates a twin Axes sharing the x-axis with an independent y-axis on the right margin, perfect for dual-metric agronomic visualizations.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> `.copy_axis()` is a non-existent Matplotlib command.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> `.twiny()` shares the y-axis and creates a twin x-axis along the top.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> `.subplot(2, 2, 2)` generates a separate adjacent grid panel, not an overlaid dual-axis chart.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Smallholder Advisory Dashboard Studio):</strong> During the Advisory Dashboard Studio, how did teams visually highlight the danger zone where soil moisture dropped below the permanent wilting point (15%)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By adding a red horizontal threshold line using ax.axhline(y=15, color=\'red\', linestyle=\'--\', label=\'Wilting Point\')</li>\n                        <li>B) By coloring all plot pixels black</li>\n                        <li>C) By deleting data points below 15%</li>\n                        <li>D) By rotating the chart upside down</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>ax.axhline(y=15, color=\'red\', linestyle=\'--\') drew an immediate visual reference line for crop wilting.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Horizontal span and line annotations (`axhline`, `axhspan`) provide critical agronomic context, allowing farmers to instantly see when sensor curves breach physiological safety thresholds.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Coloring pixels black destroys readability.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Deleting low values conceals the drought crisis.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Rotating charts produces unreadable disorientation.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Customized Farm Yield Visualization, what command ensured that date labels on the X-axis did not overlap illegibly?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) fig.autofmt_xdate() (or plt.xticks(rotation=45))</li>\n                        <li>B) plt.xlabel(\'\')</li>\n                        <li>C) plt.axis(\'off\')</li>\n                        <li>D) plt.clf()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>fig.autofmt_xdate() rotated and formatted date labels cleanly to prevent overlapping text.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> When plotting time series with daily timestamps, date strings collide; `fig.autofmt_xdate()` automatically slants date labels at 45 degrees and aligns them to tick marks for crisp readability.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Clearing the x-label leaves overlapping date tick labels unchanged.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> plt.axis(\'off\') hides the entire coordinate system, labels, and ticks.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> plt.clf() clears the entire figure.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t12': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> When comparing soil organic carbon across 5 different tillage practices, why is a Box Plot vastly more informative than a standard Bar Chart of group means?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> A bar chart shows only a single number (the mean), hiding whether the data is tightly clustered or wildly spread. A Box Plot reveals the full five-number summary: median, 25th and 75th percentiles (IQR), whiskers, and individual extreme outlier plots. It visually demonstrates whether a tillage practice reliably improves soil carbon or merely produces erratic spikes.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How does an agronomist interpret a Correlation Heatmap displaying 15 soil, weather, and crop factors simultaneously?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> A correlation heatmap encodes pairwise Pearson coefficients into color gradients (e.g., deep blue for +1.0, dark red for -1.0). An agronomist can scan the matrix in seconds to spot multi-collinearity (e.g., canopy temperature and vapor pressure deficit correlating at +0.95) and identify primary drivers of harvest yield.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Diagnostic Charting Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, what visual diagnostic chart immediately proved that nitrogen application data was heavily right-skewed with a few extreme over-fertilized outliers?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) A histogram combined with a box plot showing a long right tail and isolated outlier points beyond 1.5*IQR</li>\n                        <li>B) A pie chart with 500 identical slices</li>\n                        <li>C) An empty scatter plot</li>\n                        <li>D) A line chart with connecting dots</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>A histogram and box plot clearly displayed the right-skewed tail and extreme high-rate outliers.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Combining histograms (showing frequency density) with box plots (displaying IQR bounds and outlier markers) provides the definitive visual assessment of distributional skewness and extreme values.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Pie charts with hundreds of slices are unreadable and cannot show continuous distributions.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Empty scatter plots display no data.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Line charts imply continuous chronological sequences, inappropriate for unordered plot samples.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Farm Analytics Dashboard Studio):</strong> During the Dashboard Design Studio, what Seaborn heatmap setting displayed exact numeric correlation coefficients directly inside each colored grid cell?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sns.heatmap(corr_matrix, annot=True, cmap=\'coolwarm\', fmt=\'.2f\')</li>\n                        <li>B) sns.heatmap(corr_matrix, labels=False)</li>\n                        <li>C) sns.lineplot(corr_matrix)</li>\n                        <li>D) sns.pairplot(corr_matrix)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>sns.heatmap(..., annot=True, fmt=\'.2f\') overlaid formatted numeric correlation values inside cells.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Setting `annot=True` instructs Seaborn to print the actual correlation values inside every cell, with `fmt=\'.2f\'` formatting decimals to 2 places for precise evaluation.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> `labels=False` hides text annotations.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> sns.lineplot is for 1D/2D line trends, not 2D correlation matrices.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> sns.pairplot generates a matrix of pairwise scatterplots, not a correlation heatmap.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a 4-Panel Farm Analytics Dashboard, which Matplotlib method initialized a 2x2 grid of subplots sharing a unified figure canvas?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(12, 10))</li>\n                        <li>B) plt.figure(4)</li>\n                        <li>C) plt.grid(True)</li>\n                        <li>D) plt.axes([0, 0, 1, 1])</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(12, 10)) initialized a 2x2 array of subplot axes.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `plt.subplots(nrows, ncols)` returns both the top-level figure and a NumPy array of Axes objects (indexed via `axes[0, 0]`, `axes[0, 1]`, etc.), providing clean modular access to each panel.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> plt.figure(4) creates an empty figure window labeled 4.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> plt.grid() toggles background grid lines on the current axis.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> plt.axes() manually positions a single arbitrary axis box.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t13': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is a Seaborn Violin Plot superior to a traditional box plot when analyzing yield across three different seed varieties?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> A box plot assumes unimodal distributions within the IQR box. If a seed variety has a bimodal yield distribution (e.g., performing excellently under irrigation but failing under dry spells), a box plot hides this entirely! A Violin Plot combines a box plot with a rotated Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) curve, visually showing the exact peaks, valleys, and probability density across yields.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How does Seaborn\'s `pairplot` assist an agro-data scientist in spotting non-linear crop interactions before building machine learning models?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> `pairplot` automatically renders a grid of scatter plots for every pairwise combination of features alongside univariate distribution histograms on the diagonal. In one glance, an analyst can spot non-linear parabolic responses (e.g., temperature vs yield), clustering tendencies, and collinear features.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Seaborn Report Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, what did a Seaborn violin plot reveal about crop yield distributions that a standard box plot failed to show?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) It revealed a distinct bimodal (twin-peaked) distribution in rainfed plots caused by a split planting date</li>\n                        <li>B) It proved that all farmers had identical incomes</li>\n                        <li>C) It showed that soil moisture had negative values</li>\n                        <li>D) It printed the farmer\'s phone numbers</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Revealed a bimodal distribution with twin density peaks that box plots concealed.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Violin plots estimate kernel density along the vertical distribution axis, showing multiple modes (peaks), neck pinch points, and skewed distribution shapes that box plot rectangles hide.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Violin plots visualize data dispersion, not uniform equality.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Biological bimodality does not imply negative moisture readings.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Statistical charts do not print contact lists.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Statistical Report Design Studio):</strong> During the Report Design Studio, what Seaborn chart type was chosen to evaluate linear regression trends between nitrogen application and yield while categorizing plots by \'irrigation_type\' using color hues?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sns.lmplot(x=\'nitrogen\', y=\'yield\', hue=\'irrigation_type\', data=df)</li>\n                        <li>B) sns.barplot(x=\'nitrogen\', data=df)</li>\n                        <li>C) sns.heatmap(df)</li>\n                        <li>D) sns.rugplot(df[\'yield\'])</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>sns.lmplot(x=\'nitrogen\', y=\'yield\', hue=\'irrigation_type\', data=df) fitted multi-category linear regressions.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Seaborn\'s `lmplot()` fits linear regression lines with 95% bootstrap confidence intervals across categorical subsets designated by `hue`, allowing instant comparison of slope gradients across farming practices.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> barplot shows categorical aggregated means, not continuous linear regression trends.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> heatmap requires a 2D matrix, not raw scatter records.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> rugplot draws tick marks on an axis for marginal univariate distributions.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Seaborn Insight Report, which high-level function plotted pairwise bivariate distributions across all numeric soil and crop features in a single call?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sns.pairplot(df, hue=\'crop_type\', diag_kind=\'kde\')</li>\n                        <li>B) sns.relplot(df)</li>\n                        <li>C) sns.catplot(df)</li>\n                        <li>D) sns.jointplot(df)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>sns.pairplot(df, hue=\'crop_type\', diag_kind=\'kde\') rendered an exploratory matrix of pairwise plots.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Seaborn\'s `pairplot()` generates an (N x N) grid of scatterplots for every feature pair with KDE density curves along the diagonal, partitioned by categorical group hues.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> relplot plots specified (x, y) relationships, not all pairwise combinations.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> catplot is dedicated to categorical variables.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> jointplot evaluates exactly two variables at a time.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t14': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> What is \'Data Leakage\' in an agricultural machine learning pipeline, and why does it cause models that achieve 99% accuracy in Colab to fail completely in the field?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Data leakage occurs when information from the target or test dataset inadvertently leaks into the training pipeline before model training (e.g., scaling the entire dataset with `fit_transform` before splitting into train/test). The model learns test set statistics during training. When deployed on unseen future crops, accuracy collapses because future data was never truly held out.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why must the agricultural ML workflow always start with Problem Scoping and Agronomic Validation rather than jumping straight into algorithm selection?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> A machine learning model can optimize any mathematical loss function, but without agronomic scoping, you may optimize the wrong target! For example, predicting maximum harvest biomass rather than commercial grain yield, or predicting yield without considering cost of inputs. Clear problem scoping defines metrics aligned with real-world farm economics and actionable interventions.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Pipeline Leakage Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of agricultural data leakage, what fatal methodological error artificially inflated test R² from 0.65 to 0.98?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Fitting the StandardScaler on the entire dataset before performing the train-test split, leaking test distribution parameters (mean and variance) into the training process</li>\n                        <li>B) Using Python 3.10 instead of Python 3.8</li>\n                        <li>C) Splitting data 80/20</li>\n                        <li>D) Training a linear regression model instead of deep learning</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Fitting the scaler on the full dataset before splitting leaked test statistics into the training fold.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Data preprocessing transformations (imputation, scaling) must learn statistics strictly from the training partition (`fit_transform` on train) and only transform the test set (`transform` on test) to prevent data leakage.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Python minor version differences do not cause mathematical data leakage.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> An 80/20 split is standard practice; the error was pre-split transformation.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Algorithm choice has no bearing on pre-split scaling leakage.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Pipeline Forensic Audit Syndicate):</strong> During the Pipeline Flaw Detection Challenge, what critical flaw did student teams discover in a model predicting sugarcane harvest maturity?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) The training dataset included \'mill_delivery_weight\', a variable only recorded at the factory days after harvest, making it unavailable during field prediction</li>\n                        <li>B) The target variable was numeric</li>\n                        <li>C) The features were normalized using min-max scaling</li>\n                        <li>D) The model was evaluated using RMSE</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Included \'mill_delivery_weight\', a post-harvest feature unavailable at prediction runtime.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> A classic form of target leakage: using features that are collected chronologically after the event being predicted. At inference time in the field, factory delivery weight does not yet exist.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Continuous regression targets are expected to be numeric.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Min-max scaling is valid if fitted strictly on training data.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> RMSE is a standard, appropriate evaluation metric for continuous regression.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Miniature ML Workflow, what Scikit-Learn class encapsulated feature scaling and model training into an atomic, leak-free pipeline?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline</li>\n                        <li>B) sklearn.model_selection.GridSearchCV alone</li>\n                        <li>C) sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score</li>\n                        <li>D) sklearn.preprocessing.OneHotEncoder</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline chained preprocessing transformers and estimators into a single object.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Scikit-Learn\'s `Pipeline` guarantees that feature scaling is re-fitted strictly on training folds during cross-validation, mathematically preventing data leakage across all workflow steps.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> GridSearchCV performs hyperparameter tuning, but requires a Pipeline to prevent internal fold leakage.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> accuracy_score is a metric function, not a workflow container.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> OneHotEncoder is a single categorical transformer.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t15': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is classifying drone imagery into \'Weed vs Crop\' a supervised learning task, while segmenting a 5,000-acre farm into \'Management Zones\' is an unsupervised task?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Weed classification has explicit ground truth: human agronomists label bounding boxes or pixels as \'Broadleaf Weed\' or \'Soybean Seedling\'. Management zoning has no predefined true labels; the algorithm performs unsupervised clustering (e.g., K-Means on EC, slope, and organic matter) to discover natural soil boundaries without human preconceptions.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Can you use unsupervised clustering to improve a downstream supervised crop yield predictor? How?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Yes! This is \'Semi-Supervised\' or \'Feature Engineering\': an unsupervised algorithm (like K-Means or PCA) discovers soil zones or compresses 20 spectral bands into 3 principal components. These cluster IDs or principal components are then fed as input features into a supervised regression model, boosting predictive accuracy.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Dual-Pipeline Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration comparing a weed classifier with soil zoning, what was the primary structural difference in how data was formatted for training?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) The supervised weed classifier required input features X paired with ground-truth target labels y, while the unsupervised soil clustering model received only feature matrix X</li>\n                        <li>B) The unsupervised model required labeled target strings</li>\n                        <li>C) The supervised model could not use numbers</li>\n                        <li>D) The unsupervised model ran only on quantum hardware</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Supervised training required both features X and target labels y; unsupervised clustering required only X.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Supervised learning models learn mathematical functions mapping inputs X to known labels y (`model.fit(X, y)`). Unsupervised models discover patterns in inputs X without target labels (`model.fit(X)`).</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Unsupervised models do not use target labels.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Supervised models natively process numerical pixel tensors.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> K-Means runs on standard CPUs and GPUs.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Learning Paradigm Selection Studio):</strong> During the Paradigm Selection Studio, a farmer cooperative requested an AI system to group 10,000 member farmers into credit-risk clusters without having historical default records. Which approach was selected?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Unsupervised clustering (K-Means / DBSCAN) based on land size, crop diversity, and irrigation assets to segment farmers by structural capability</li>\n                        <li>B) Supervised binary logistic regression guessing fake default labels</li>\n                        <li>C) Reinforcement learning with cash penalties for farmers</li>\n                        <li>D) Deleting the cooperative database</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Unsupervised clustering (K-Means/DBSCAN) based on structural farm assets and diversity.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> When historical ground-truth target labels (default history) do not exist, supervised classification is mathematically impossible. Unsupervised clustering groups members by multivariate asset and operational similarity.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Inventing fake labels invalidates supervised learning and creates legal liability.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> RL with real cash penalties is unethical and financially disastrous.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Deleting databases destroys enterprise operations.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building and comparing both paradigms, what Scikit-Learn method generated cluster assignments for the unlabeled soil dataset?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) kmeans.fit_predict(X_soil)</li>\n                        <li>B) kmeans.predict_proba(X_soil)</li>\n                        <li>C) kmeans.score_residuals(X_soil)</li>\n                        <li>D) kmeans.cross_validate()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>kmeans.fit_predict(X_soil) fitted centroids and returned cluster integer assignments.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `fit_predict()` fits cluster centroids on feature matrix X and returns cluster ID assignments (0, 1, 2...) for each row in a single convenient step.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Standard K-Means does not provide class probabilities (`predict_proba`).</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> score_residuals is not a Scikit-Learn clustering method.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> cross_validate is for supervised model evaluation.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t16': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why does a random train-test split (`train_test_split(shuffle=True)`) cause disastrous spatial data leakage when evaluating a crop disease detection model across adjacent farm plots?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Nearby farm plots share spatial autocorrelation (Tobler\'s First Law of Geography: \'everything is related to everything else, but near things are more related than distant things\'). If plot A is in the train set and adjacent plot B is in the test set, the model memorizes the local soil, lighting, and seed variety. It gets 99% test accuracy but fails completely when deployed on a new farm 20 km away. Spatial block splitting must be used.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> In agricultural yield forecasting, why must the test set always be drawn from future growing seasons rather than random days within the same year?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Temporal autocorrelation! Randomly splitting days leaks future weather into the past (e.g., training on Tuesday, testing on Wednesday, training on Thursday). In production, an AI model trained on historical seasons must predict the *next* unseen year\'s harvest without knowing future weather.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Spatial Leakage Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, what happened when crop yield data was split using random shuffling versus spatial-block splitting by distinct geographic taluks?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Random shuffling achieved an artificially high R² of 0.94 due to spatial autocorrelation leakage, while spatial-block splitting revealed the true generalization R² was only 0.68</li>\n                        <li>B) Spatial-block splitting crashed the computer</li>\n                        <li>C) Random shuffling was 100% accurate in physical field validation</li>\n                        <li>D) Both splits yielded identical performance</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Random shuffling produced inflated R² (0.94) via spatial leakage; spatial splitting exposed true generalization (0.68).</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Spatial autocorrelation leaks information between adjacent training and test plots. Evaluating on geographically separated held-out blocks provides an honest assessment of model generalization to new farms.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Spatial splitting is a grouping method and does not crash hardware.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Models with spatial leakage fail in real-world deployments.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Due to spatial correlation, random splits always overestimate generalization.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Multi-Season Validation Studio):</strong> During the Validation Strategy Studio, how did teams structure the validation split for a 5-year agricultural dataset (2020–2024)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Time-series split: Training on 2020–2023 historical seasons and testing strictly on the held-out 2024 season to evaluate future forecasting</li>\n                        <li>B) Randomly shuffling all 5 years together</li>\n                        <li>C) Training on 2024 and testing on 2020</li>\n                        <li>D) Deleting the 2024 data</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Temporal split: trained on 2020–2023 and tested strictly on the held-out future 2024 season.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Temporal out-of-time validation mirrors real-world deployment: models learn historical multi-season trends and must predict future harvests without hindsight leakage.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Random shuffling across years creates look-ahead temporal leakage.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Predicting past historical data from future training data reverses temporal causality.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Deleting held-out test years eliminates validation rigor.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task implementing a 3-way Stratified Time-Aware Split, what Scikit-Learn parameter preserved the proportion of rare crop disease cases across train and validation sets?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) stratify=y in train_test_split()</li>\n                        <li>B) shuffle=False</li>\n                        <li>C) random_state=None</li>\n                        <li>D) test_size=0.99</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>stratify=y preserved the exact class proportions across training and test subsets.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Stratification ensures that rare target classes (e.g., a severe crop virus present in only 2% of samples) are represented in identical proportions in both train and test splits, preventing empty test classes.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> shuffle=False maintains chronological order but does not guarantee balanced class representation.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> random_state=None creates non-reproducible randomized splits.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> test_size=0.99 leaves almost no data for training.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t17': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is standard 5-Fold Cross Validation inadequate for agricultural time series and geospatial data?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Standard K-Fold randomly shuffles samples into folds, guaranteeing spatial and temporal leakage. A training fold will contain plots immediately adjacent to test plots, or days wedged between test days. Agricultural CV requires structured partitioning: GroupKFold (grouped by farm or district) or TimeSeriesSplit (expanding historical windows).</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What is \'Purged Group Time-Series Cross-Validation\', and why is it used in commodity mandi price forecasting?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> In financial and mandi price modeling, prices exhibit autocorrelation across days. If an event occurs on day 10, its market shock echoes through day 15. Purging inserts an embargo window (e.g., 5 days) between training folds and test folds to prevent residual autoregressive memory from leaking across boundaries.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Spatial Block CV Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, what Scikit-Learn cross-validator ensured that all sensor readings from the same farm plot remained together in the same fold?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) GroupKFold(n_splits=5) passing groups=df[\'plot_id\']</li>\n                        <li>B) KFold(shuffle=True)</li>\n                        <li>C) StratifiedKFold without groups</li>\n                        <li>D) LeaveOneOut() with random permutation</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>GroupKFold(n_splits=5) passing groups=df[\'plot_id\'] kept individual plots intact within folds.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `GroupKFold` guarantees that no group (e.g., a specific farm plot or village) is split across both train and test partitions in any fold, testing true out-of-farm generalization.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Standard KFold splits rows randomly, mixing readings from the same plot into both train and test.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> StratifiedKFold balances class labels but ignores group clustering.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> LeaveOneOut without group constraints leaks plot context on repeated measurements.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Spatial Autocorrelation Audit Syndicate):</strong> During the Spatial Leakage Audit, what metric dropped significantly when student teams shifted from standard KFold to spatial GroupKFold across 8 agro-climatic zones?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Cross-validated R² dropped from an over-optimistic 0.91 to a realistic 0.72, exposing spatial over-fitting</li>\n                        <li>B) Model training time increased to 48 hours</li>\n                        <li>C) The number of rows in the dataset decreased by 50%</li>\n                        <li>D) All coefficients became negative</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>CV R² dropped from 0.91 to 0.72, exposing spatial over-fitting and revealing genuine generalization.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Spatial GroupKFold exposes the true difficulty of predicting on unseen geographical regions, stripping away the artificial score inflation caused by spatial proximity.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> GroupKFold has the same computational complexity as standard KFold.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Cross-validation partitions data without discarding rows.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Realistic evaluation does not arbitrarily invert model coefficient signs.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task implementing TimeSeriesSplit on daily mandi prices, how did the training window evolve across consecutive folds?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) It expanded incrementally forward in time (Fold 1: train on month 1, test on month 2; Fold 2: train on months 1-2, test on month 3...)</li>\n                        <li>B) It randomly sampled days across all 12 months</li>\n                        <li>C) It trained only on the last day of the year</li>\n                        <li>D) It moved backwards from December to January</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>The training window expanded forward chronologically (walk-forward validation) without look-ahead bias.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Scikit-Learn\'s `TimeSeriesSplit` implements rolling walk-forward validation: each successive fold trains on all available historical data up to time t and evaluates on time t+1.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Random sampling across time series introduces look-ahead bias.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Training on a single day provides insufficient historical data.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Moving backwards violates temporal causality.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t18': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> A complex deep neural network achieves 99.9% training accuracy on a small dataset of 200 rice disease photos, but drops to 45% on photos taken by farmers in the field. What happened?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Severe Overfitting (High Variance)! With 200 photos, the complex network has millions of parameters and memorized specific background details (e.g., shadows, soil color, fingernails holding leaves) rather than invariant biological lesion features. When shown new farmer photos with different backgrounds, the overfit model fails completely.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why is a simple linear model that underfits crop yield (High Bias) just as dangerous for a farmer as an overfit model?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> An underfit model is too simplistic to capture real biological dynamics (e.g., assuming crop yield grows infinitely with nitrogen fertilizer). It prescribes toxic fertilizer amounts because it cannot represent the curved law of diminishing returns, leading to crop burn and economic waste.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Polynomial Degree Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration fitting crop yield response curves, what happened when polynomial feature degree was increased from degree 2 (quadratic) to degree 15?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) The degree 15 model oscillated wildly between training points, achieving 0.0 training loss but catastrophic test error (severe overfitting)</li>\n                        <li>B) The degree 15 model became a straight horizontal line</li>\n                        <li>C) The training loss increased to infinity</li>\n                        <li>D) The model automatically deleted redundant features</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Degree 15 model oscillated wildly, achieving zero training loss but catastrophic test error.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> High-degree polynomials have extreme variance: they contort to pass through every individual training point (including noise and outliers), producing wild fluctuations that generalize terribly to new data.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Degree 1 (linear) is a straight line; high-degree polynomials are highly curved and oscillatory.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Higher model complexity consistently reduces or zeroes out training loss.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Unregularized high-degree polynomials retain all polynomial terms without pruning.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Learning Curve Diagnostic Studio):</strong> During the Learning Curve Diagnostic Studio, what did a plot showing a large, persistent gap between high training score (98%) and low validation score (62%) diagnose?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) High Variance (Overfitting) &mdash; the model memorized the training set and failed to generalize to validation data</li>\n                        <li>B) High Bias (Underfitting)</li>\n                        <li>C) Perfect model convergence</li>\n                        <li>D) Data collection completed successfully</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>High Variance (Overfitting) indicated by a wide gap between training score and validation score.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In learning curve diagnostics, a wide divergence between training performance and validation performance is the hallmark of high variance / overfitting. Regularization, pruning, or adding training data is required.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> High bias shows both training and validation curves converging at low accuracy.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Convergence occurs when train and validation curves meet at a high performance plateau.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Learning curves diagnose algorithm bias/variance, not data collection completeness.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Bias-Variance Tradeoff Experiment, how did Ridge (L2) regression cure the overfitting of the high-degree polynomial model?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By adding a squared penalty on coefficient weights (alpha * sum(w²)) to the loss function, shrinking weights and smoothing the curve</li>\n                        <li>B) By multiplying all feature values by 10</li>\n                        <li>C) By deleting all input data columns</li>\n                        <li>D) By rounding all predictions to integers</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Added an L2 penalty (alpha * sum(w²)) shrinking weights and curbing wild curve oscillations.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Ridge regularization penalizes large coefficient weights in the loss function, preventing the polynomial from oscillating violently between sample points and restoring generalization balance.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Multiplying features by 10 increases numerical instability without regularizing.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Deleting all data destroys the model.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Integer rounding does not prevent polynomial curve oscillation during training.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t19': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> What is the fundamental difference between a model \'parameter\' and a \'hyperparameter\' in precision agriculture algorithms?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> A *parameter* is learned automatically by the algorithm from data during training (e.g., the slope weights `w` in linear regression, or split thresholds in decision trees). A *hyperparameter* is an external configuration set by the data scientist *before* training begins (e.g., `max_depth` in a decision tree, `learning_rate` in gradient boosting). Hyperparameters dictate *how* the model learns.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why is exhaustive Grid Search (`GridSearchCV`) computationally infeasible when tuning a Random Forest on a massive national satellite dataset, and why is `RandomizedSearchCV` or Optuna preferred?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> The \'Curse of Dimensionality\'! If you test 5 parameters with 4 values each across 5-fold CV, GridSearchCV trains `4^5 * 5 = 5,120` full models. On large datasets, this takes days or weeks. RandomizedSearchCV samples a fixed budget of random parameter combinations, exploring broader parameter spaces in a fraction of the time with near-identical optimization quality.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Search Comparison Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live tuning demonstration on a Random Forest soil classifier, how did RandomizedSearchCV compare with GridSearchCV in runtime and accuracy?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) RandomizedSearchCV explored 60 diverse hyperparameter combinations in 1/8th the time of GridSearchCV, converging on an F1-score within 0.5% of exhaustive search</li>\n                        <li>B) GridSearchCV was 100x faster than RandomizedSearchCV</li>\n                        <li>C) RandomizedSearchCV generated negative accuracy scores</li>\n                        <li>D) Both methods selected identical default hyperparameters</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>RandomizedSearchCV achieved comparable accuracy in 1/8th the time by sampling parameter distributions.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Randomized search samples parameter spaces probabilistically, avoiding wasted compute on unpromising parameter neighborhoods and finding near-optimal configurations dramatically faster than exhaustive grid search.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Exhaustive grid search evaluates every combination and is vastly slower than randomized sampling.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Randomized search optimizes the same valid evaluation metrics.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Hyperparameter search algorithms discover optimal non-default parameters.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Grid Explosion Forensic Studio):</strong> During the Grid Explosion Audit, what mathematical calculation explained why a student group\'s GridSearchCV took 14 hours to run on Colab?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) 6 hyperparameters with 5 values each across 5 cross-validation folds = 5^6 * 5 = 78,125 model fits, overwhelming the CPU</li>\n                        <li>B) The dataset was saved in CSV format</li>\n                        <li>C) Colab disconnected the internet connection immediately</li>\n                        <li>D) The target variable had only two classes</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Combinatorial explosion: 5^6 * 5 = 78,125 fits overwhelmed the single-threaded CPU runtime.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Combinatorial explosion occurs when grid spaces multiply exponentially. Tuning 6 parameters across 5 candidate values each requires evaluating 15,625 configurations, multiplied by 5 folds = 78,125 individual training runs.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> CSV loading happens once and does not explain 14-hour model training loops.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> If Colab disconnected, execution would halt immediately rather than running for 14 hours.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Binary classification is faster than multiclass, not slower.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task optimizing hyperparameters with Optuna, how did Bayesian Tree-structured Parzen Estimators (TPE) outperform random search?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) By building a probabilistic model of past trial results to intelligently sample future hyperparameters in regions with highest expected improvement</li>\n                        <li>B) By testing every parameter alphabetically</li>\n                        <li>C) By running all trials simultaneously on a single CPU core</li>\n                        <li>D) By resetting all parameters to zero after every trial</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Used Bayesian probability (TPE) to guide future trials toward promising hyperparameter regions.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Optuna\'s Bayesian optimization uses past trial evaluations to model the objective function landscape, directing computational budget toward hyperparameter regions with high probability of performance gain.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Hyperparameters are numeric/categorical, not searched alphabetically.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Bayesian optimization is sequential, not brute-force concurrent single-core.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Resetting parameters to zero discards accumulated probabilistic optimization history.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t20': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is Scikit-Learn\'s consistent three-method API design (`fit()`, `transform()`, `predict()`) considered a masterpiece of machine learning software engineering?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Consistency enables modularity! Every estimator in Scikit-Learn follows the exact same blueprint: `fit(X, y)` estimates parameters from training data, `transform(X)` applies learned transformations to features, and `predict(X)` outputs predictions. This uniformity allows data scientists to swap a Linear Regression for a Random Forest or XGBoost by changing a single line of code without rewriting the pipeline.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How does Scikit-Learn\'s `ColumnTransformer` solve the problem of processing an agricultural dataset containing mixed data types (e.g., continuous numeric soil pH and categorical village names)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> A standard scaler cannot scale text strings, and an encoder cannot scale floats. `ColumnTransformer` routes specific column subsets to dedicated preprocessing pipelines: continuous numerical features pass through `StandardScaler()`, while categorical text strings pass through `OneHotEncoder()`. The transformed arrays are then concatenated side-by-side into a single matrix for modeling.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Pipeline Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, what Scikit-Learn utility routed numeric columns to StandardScaler and categorical columns to OneHotEncoder in a unified step?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sklearn.compose.ColumnTransformer</li>\n                        <li>B) sklearn.preprocessing.LabelEncoder</li>\n                        <li>C) sklearn.model_selection.train_test_split</li>\n                        <li>D) sklearn.decomposition.PCA</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>ColumnTransformer applied distinct preprocessing transformers to specified column subsets.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `ColumnTransformer` applies different transformers to designated column slices of an array or DataFrame, combining the outputs into a single numerical feature matrix for downstream estimators.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> LabelEncoder is for 1D target labels, not multi-column feature matrix transformations.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> train_test_split partitions rows, not column preprocessing.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> PCA performs dimensionality reduction, not mixed-type feature encoding.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Pipeline Architecture Studio):</strong> During the Architecture Studio, why did student teams wrap their preprocessing and classifier inside a Scikit-Learn `Pipeline` before passing it to `cross_val_score()`?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) To guarantee that preprocessing (scaling and imputation) is re-fitted strictly on training folds inside each cross-validation split, completely preventing data leakage</li>\n                        <li>B) To make the Python file smaller</li>\n                        <li>C) Because Scikit-Learn models cannot run without pipelines</li>\n                        <li>D) To convert the model to C++ automatically</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Guaranteed that preprocessing was re-fitted exclusively on training folds during CV, eliminating leakage.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Wrapping transformations and models in a `Pipeline` ensures that `fit()` and `transform()` are applied cleanly within each CV split, mathematically isolating validation data from preprocessing parameter estimation.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Pipelines add software abstraction and do not reduce file size.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Models can be run independently, but doing so manually during CV risks leakage.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Pipelines remain native Python/Cython code and do not transpile to C++.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building an End-to-End Scikit-Learn Pipeline, which method called on the fitted pipeline simultaneously transformed unseen field test data and generated crop yield predictions?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) pipeline.predict(X_test)</li>\n                        <li>B) pipeline.fit_transform(X_test)</li>\n                        <li>C) pipeline.score_test()</li>\n                        <li>D) pipeline.to_json()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>pipeline.predict(X_test) automatically applied learned transformations before model inference.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Calling `.predict()` on a fitted pipeline automatically routes raw test inputs through all intermediate `transform()` steps using previously fitted parameters before passing the array to the final estimator\'s `predict()` method.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Calling `fit_transform` on test data recalculates parameters on the test set, causing data leakage.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> `.score_test()` is a fictitious method name.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> to_json() is for serializing text, not executing model inference.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t21': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression modeled as finding a line that minimizes the sum of *squared* residuals rather than the sum of *absolute* residuals?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Squaring residuals serves two mathematical purposes: First, it treats positive and negative errors equally (canceling sign differences). Second, squaring heavily penalizes large errors (e.g., an error of 10 quintals incurs a penalty of 100, while an error of 2 incurs only 4). Furthermore, squared loss yields a smooth, continuous parabola with a closed-form analytical derivative: `(X^T X)^(-1) X^T y`.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> What does the slope coefficient (m) in a univariate linear regression equation `Yield = 2.4 * Fertilizer + 15` signify to an agronomist?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> The slope (m = 2.4) represents the marginal response rate: for every additional unit (e.g., kg/ha) of fertilizer applied, crop yield increases by an expected 2.4 quintals/ha. The intercept (15) represents baseline unfertilized soil productivity. If marginal fertilizer cost exceeds the market value of 2.4 quintals, the agronomist knows fertilizer is economically unviable.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live OLS Fitting Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration fitting Ordinary Least Squares to cotton yield, what diagnostic residual plot confirmed that the linear regression assumptions were satisfied?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) A residual vs predicted plot showing random scatter around zero with constant variance (homoscedasticity) and no curved patterns</li>\n                        <li>B) A residual plot forming a distinct U-shaped curve</li>\n                        <li>C) Residuals that increased exponentially with yield</li>\n                        <li>D) A plot where all residuals were exactly equal to zero</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Residuals scattered randomly around zero with constant variance (homoscedasticity) and no curvature.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Valid OLS regression requires that residuals are normally distributed with constant variance (homoscedasticity) and exhibit no non-linear patterns. A random horizontal scatter band around zero confirms model adequacy.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> A U-shaped residual pattern indicates unmodeled non-linearity requiring polynomial terms.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Expanding residuals indicate heteroscedasticity, violating OLS assumptions.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Exactly zero residuals indicate pure interpolation or severe overfitting.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Multicollinearity Studio):</strong> During the Regression Assumptions Studio, what diagnostic metric flagged that \'soil_moisture_pct\' and \'soil_water_tension_bars\' were collinear, causing unstable regression coefficients?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) exceeding 10.0 for both variables</li>\n                        <li>B) The p-value was exactly 0.50</li>\n                        <li>C) The R-squared was negative</li>\n                        <li>D) The number of observations was odd</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) > 10.0 flagged severe multicollinearity between moisture metrics.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> VIF measures how much the variance of an estimated regression coefficient increases when features are correlated. A VIF > 5–10 indicates severe multicollinearity, requiring dropping or combining redundant features.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> A p-value of 0.50 indicates statistical non-significance, not multicollinearity.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Multicollinearity typically inflates R², it does not make it negative.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Odd or even observation counts have zero statistical connection to multicollinearity.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Crop Yield Predictor, which Scikit-Learn attributes extracted the learned feature slope weights and baseline intercept?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) model.coef_ and model.intercept_</li>\n                        <li>B) model.weights_ and model.bias_</li>\n                        <li>C) model.slopes_ and model.origin_</li>\n                        <li>D) model.parameters_()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>model.coef_ holds the slope coefficient array and model.intercept_ holds the scalar intercept.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Following Scikit-Learn convention, attributes learned during `fit()` end with a trailing underscore. In `LinearRegression`, `coef_` stores feature slopes and `intercept_` stores the baseline intercept.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> weights_ and bias_ are naming conventions used in deep learning frameworks like PyTorch, not Scikit-Learn linear models.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> slopes_ and origin_ are non-existent attributes.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> parameters_() is invalid syntax.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t22': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) more sensitive to catastrophic agricultural prediction errors than Mean Absolute Error (MAE)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> MAE treats all errors linearly (an error of 10 quintals is penalized exactly twice as much as an error of 5). RMSE squares the errors before averaging: an error of 10 is penalized 100, while an error of 5 is penalized 25 (4x higher!). In commercial agriculture, predicting harvest yield off by a massive margin triggers severe supply chain default penalties; RMSE heavily penalizes these disastrous large deviations.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> Why does adding 20 useless, random noise features to a linear regression model cause standard R² to increase, and why does Adjusted R² prevent this delusion?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Mathematical mechanics of OLS! Adding any new feature—even random lottery numbers—gives the optimization slight degrees of freedom to fit random sample noise, mechanically decreasing residual sum of squares and increasing standard R². Adjusted R² penalizes model complexity by dividing by degrees of freedom: if a new feature does not improve the model more than random chance, Adjusted R² decreases.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Metric Demonstration):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live evaluation of potato yield predictions, why was RMSE reported alongside MAE when comparing a baseline linear model against a gradient boosted regressor?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) RMSE penalizes large outlier errors quadratically, revealing that the baseline model had occasional catastrophic mispredictions that MAE masked</li>\n                        <li>B) RMSE is always smaller than MAE</li>\n                        <li>C) MAE only works on integer numbers</li>\n                        <li>D) RMSE converts yield units from quintals into kilograms</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>RMSE penalizes large errors quadratically, exposing extreme mispredictions that MAE conceals.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Because RMSE squares errors before taking the root, large discrepancies pull RMSE significantly above MAE. A large gap between RMSE and MAE indicates the presence of severe prediction outliers.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Mathematically, RMSE is always greater than or equal to MAE due to Jensen\'s inequality.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> MAE operates seamlessly on continuous floating-point predictions.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> RMSE retains the exact same physical measurement units as the target variable (quintals).</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Metric Selection Studio):</strong> During the Metric Trade-off Studio, why did student teams choose Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) to communicate model performance to cooperative smallholder farmers?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) MAPE expresses prediction error as an intuitive percentage (e.g., \'on average, predictions are within 8% of actual harvest\'), making it understandable without statistical training</li>\n                        <li>B) MAPE is the only metric supported by Python</li>\n                        <li>C) MAPE guarantees zero prediction errors</li>\n                        <li>D) MAPE is immune to division-by-zero errors when yields are 0</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>MAPE communicates errors as an intuitive percentage of actual harvest volume.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> While data scientists rely on RMSE and R², non-technical agricultural stakeholders and farmers intuitively understand percentage error (e.g., \'our forecast is accurate to within ±7% of your final yield\').</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Python supports dozens of regression metrics via Scikit-Learn.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> No evaluation metric guarantees zero error.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> MAPE divides by actual values, making it undefined or explosive when actual yield is zero.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Regression Metrics Calculator, which Scikit-Learn function computed the coefficient of determination (R²)?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sklearn.metrics.r2_score(y_true, y_pred)</li>\n                        <li>B) sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score(y_true, y_pred)</li>\n                        <li>C) sklearn.metrics.f1_score(y_true, y_pred)</li>\n                        <li>D) sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score(y_true, y_pred)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>r2_score(y_true, y_pred) computed the proportion of variance explained by the regression model.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `r2_score` calculates the coefficient of determination (1 - SS_res / SS_tot), measuring the proportion of target variance explained by model features.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> accuracy_score is strictly for categorical classification.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> f1_score evaluates harmonic precision/recall in classification.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> roc_auc_score evaluates binary classification ranking.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t23': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why can\'t we use standard Linear Regression to predict whether a crop plot is infected with bacterial wilt (Infected = 1, Healthy = 0)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Linear regression fits an unbounded straight line (`y = mx + b`). For extreme feature values (e.g., very high humidity), it predicts probabilities of 1.4 or -0.3—which are mathematically impossible! Logistic Regression maps the linear combination through the non-linear Sigmoid function, bounding predictions strictly between 0.0 and 1.0 as valid probabilities.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> In agricultural disease quarantine, why should the classification decision threshold be lowered from the default 0.50 to 0.25?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Asymmetric error costs! A False Negative (failing to detect an infected crop) allows an infectious pathogen to spread across the entire district, causing total agricultural quarantine and millions in losses. A False Positive (inspecting a healthy plot) incurs only minor lab testing cost. Lowering the decision threshold to 0.25 prioritizes recall, catching every possible infection.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Sigmoid & Threshold Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration of logistic regression on crop fungal stress, what mathematical function compressed the unbounded linear equation z = w*x + b into a [0, 1] probability range?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) The Sigmoid (logistic) function: σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z))</li>\n                        <li>B) The ReLU function: max(0, z)</li>\n                        <li>C) The Tangent function: tan(z)</li>\n                        <li>D) The absolute value function: |z|</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>The Sigmoid function σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z)) mapped real numbers into valid [0, 1] probabilities.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> The Sigmoid function creates an S-shaped curve that asymptotically approaches 0 at negative infinity and 1 at positive infinity, converting log-odds into well-calibrated probabilities.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> ReLU outputs unbounded positive values from 0 to infinity, not bounded probabilities.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Tangent has vertical asymptotes and oscillates across negative/positive infinity.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Absolute value is unbounded and cannot model binary probability transitions.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Farm Cost Asymmetry Studio):</strong> During the Farm Cost Asymmetry Studio, why did student teams adjust the logistic regression classification threshold from 0.50 down to 0.20 for detecting locust swarm infestation?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Because missing a locust swarm (False Negative) leads to catastrophic crop destruction, so the model was tuned for maximum Recall over Precision</li>\n                        <li>B) To increase model execution speed on the CPU</li>\n                        <li>C) Because locusts can only be detected at 0.20 probability</li>\n                        <li>D) To reduce the number of true positive detections</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Tuned threshold to 0.20 to maximize Recall, prioritizing early swarm detection over false alarms.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> In biosecurity and disaster prevention, the cost of a False Negative vastly outweighs a False Positive. Lowering the decision threshold forces the classifier to trigger alerts even on weak warning signs.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Threshold tuning changes decision boundaries on predicted probabilities; it does not change CPU inference speed.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Probabilities are continuous; locusts do not carry fixed probability values.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Lowering thresholds increases true positives and false positives.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Logistic Regression Classifier, which method returned the continuous class probabilities instead of hard binary (0/1) predictions?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) model.predict_proba(X_test)</li>\n                        <li>B) model.predict(X_test)</li>\n                        <li>C) model.decision_function(X_test)</li>\n                        <li>D) model.score(X_test)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>model.predict_proba(X_test) returned a 2D array of class probabilities for classes 0 and 1.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `predict_proba()` returns the raw Sigmoid probability scores (e.g., [0.15, 0.85]), allowing data scientists to evaluate custom decision thresholds and compute ROC-AUC curves.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> `predict()` applies the fixed default 0.5 threshold and outputs hard categorical labels (0 or 1).</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> `decision_function()` returns unscaled log-odds distances to the decision hyperplane.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> `score()` returns overall accuracy.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t24': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why are Decision Trees beloved by field agronomists and agricultural extension officers over \'black-box\' deep neural networks?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Interpretability! A decision tree can be drawn as an intuitive flowchart of IF-THEN rules: `if soil_ph < 5.5 and drainage == \'poor\' -> Waterlogging Acidosis`. An agronomist can inspect every branch, verify that the tree\'s logic aligns with plant physiology, and explain the diagnosis to a farmer in plain local language.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> If an unpruned Decision Tree is allowed to grow to infinite depth on a farm dataset of 500 plots, why will it achieve 100% training accuracy but fail miserably in the field?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> An unconstrained tree will keep splitting until every single training sample occupies its own leaf node, creating complex decision boundaries that isolate individual noisy outliers (e.g., memorizing that Plot 42 with pH 6.1 on a Tuesday had mildew). Pruning (`max_depth`, `min_samples_split`) enforces Occam\'s Razor, keeping only generalizable rules.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Tree Pruning Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration, how did pruning a Decision Tree (setting max_depth=4 and min_samples_leaf=10) affect its performance on unlabelled test plots?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) It eliminated over-complex leaf splits, reducing training accuracy slightly but dramatically increasing test accuracy on unseen farms</li>\n                        <li>B) It reduced both training and test accuracy to zero</li>\n                        <li>C) It converted the decision tree into a neural network</li>\n                        <li>D) It increased tree depth to 100 levels</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Pruning prevented overfitting on noisy outliers, trading slight training drop for higher test generalization.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Unconstrained decision trees overfit by memorizing training noise. Constraining tree growth via `max_depth` and `min_samples_leaf` regularizes the tree, enforcing simpler, more robust biological decision rules.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Pruning regularizes models; it does not destroy accuracy.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Pruning restricts tree node expansion and has nothing to do with neural networks.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Setting max_depth=4 caps tree depth at 4, preventing deeper growth.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Decision Rule Extraction Studio):</strong> During the Tree Interpretability Studio, which metric did the Decision Tree algorithm minimize at each node split to evaluate partition purity?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Gini Impurity (or Entropy / Information Gain)</li>\n                        <li>B) R-squared</li>\n                        <li>C) Euclidean distance</li>\n                        <li>D) Mean Absolute Percentage Error</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Gini Impurity (or Entropy) measured class label homogeneity across candidate feature splits.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Classification trees evaluate candidate splits by measuring the reduction in impurity (Gini or Entropy), selecting the feature and threshold that produces the purest, most homogeneous child subsets.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> R-squared evaluates continuous regression, not categorical classification splits.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Euclidean distance is used in K-Means clustering and KNN, not decision tree splits.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> MAPE evaluates continuous percentage errors.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task building a Pruned Decision Tree, which Scikit-Learn function visualized the trained tree as a graphical flowchart of decision nodes?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sklearn.tree.plot_tree(clf, feature_names=features, filled=True)</li>\n                        <li>B) clf.show_tree()</li>\n                        <li>C) plt.imshow(clf)</li>\n                        <li>D) clf.export_flowchart()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>sklearn.tree.plot_tree(clf, filled=True) rendered the hierarchical node flowchart with color coding.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `plot_tree()` renders the decision nodes, split criteria, sample counts, and dominant class colors directly onto a Matplotlib figure, providing full visual transparency into model reasoning.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> show_tree() is a fictitious method.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> plt.imshow() expects a 2D/3D image pixel array, not an estimator object.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> export_flowchart is not a valid Scikit-Learn function.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}, 'm2-t25': {'reflexive': '            <!-- 💭 Reflexive Questions (Pre-Topic Discovery) -->\n            <div class="reflexive-questions">\n                <div class="reflexive-header">\n                    <h2><i class="fas fa-brain"></i> 🤔 Reflexive Questions (Think Before You Learn)</h2>\n                    <p class="reflexive-subtitle">Pause and ponder these real-world dilemmas before exploring the concepts. Formulate your intuition, then click to reveal the deeper insight.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 1:</strong> Why is Mean Decrease in Impurity (MDI / Gini Importance) dangerously biased when analyzing agricultural datasets that contain high-cardinality features (like unique plot IDs or dates)?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> MDI evaluates how often a feature is chosen for splitting across tree nodes. High-cardinality features (like unique plot IDs or timestamps) offer countless unique numerical split points, allowing the tree to use them for easy greedy impurity reduction. MDI artificially inflates their importance to the top, even though unique IDs have zero predictive generalization on new farms! Permutation Importance solves this.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                <div class="reflexive-card">\n                    <p class="reflexive-prompt"><strong>🤔 Reflection 2:</strong> How does Permutation Importance evaluate whether \'Soil Organic Carbon\' is genuinely critical for crop yield prediction?</p>\n                    <details class="reflexive-details">\n                        <summary><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Click to Reveal Perspective &amp; Intuition</summary>\n                        <div class="reflexive-answer">\n                            <p><strong>Agronomic &amp; Computational Insight:</strong> Permutation Importance evaluates the model on held-out validation data. It takes the \'Soil Organic Carbon\' column and randomly shuffles (permutes) its values, breaking its relationship with crop yield while keeping all other columns intact. If model prediction error surges dramatically after shuffling, Soil Organic Carbon is genuinely critical; if error barely changes, the feature was redundant.</p>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n', 'quiz': '            <div class="quiz-section">\n                <h2>📝 Knowledge Check: Activity-Based Feedback MCQs</h2>\n                <p class="quiz-intro">Test your understanding of the simulations, multi-tier architectures, and edge cases examined in this topic\'s Learn by Doing hub.</p>\n                \n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q1 (Teacher Live Importance Demo):</strong> In the Teacher\'s live demonstration comparing MDI against Permutation Importance, what occurred when a random noise column was added to the farm dataset?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Tree-based MDI incorrectly assigned substantial importance to the noise feature, while Permutation Importance correctly scored its importance as zero</li>\n                        <li>B) Both methods gave the noise column 100% importance</li>\n                        <li>C) Permutation importance crashed the runtime</li>\n                        <li>D) The random noise column improved crop yield by 20%</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>MDI over-valued the random noise column, while Permutation Importance correctly gave it near-zero importance.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Default tree feature importance (MDI) is biased toward continuous or high-cardinality noise features. Permutation importance tests real performance drops on validation data, accurately exposing useless noise.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Permutation importance tests on held-out data and rejects noise.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Permutation testing is standard shuffle-and-predict computation and runs reliably.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Adding synthetic noise columns has no biological effect on physical crop yield.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q2 (Agronomic Plausibility Studio):</strong> During the Domain Validation Studio, an AI model flagged \'farmer_favorite_color\' as the second most important feature predicting harvest yield. What corrective action was taken?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) Removing the spurious feature immediately and enforcing agronomic domain plausibility screening before model finalization</li>\n                        <li>B) Publishing the finding that color directly alters plant photosynthesis</li>\n                        <li>C) Painting all tractors with the favorite color to boost harvest</li>\n                        <li>D) Multiplying the color column by 100</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>Removed the spurious feature and instituted agronomic domain plausibility screening.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> Spurious statistical correlations occur regularly in small agricultural datasets. Machine learning models must always undergo domain plausibility validation with agronomists to prune confounding or nonsensical features.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> Farmer favorite color has zero causal relationship with plant cellular biology.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> Painting tractors based on spurious correlation is wasteful and scientifically absurd.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> Scaling spurious features exacerbates model error.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class="quiz-question">\n                    <p><strong>Q3 (Individual Colab Practice):</strong> In your Individual Colab task computing Permutation Importance, which Scikit-Learn function calculated the metric on the validation dataset?</p>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>A) sklearn.inspection.permutation_importance(model, X_val, y_val, n_repeats=10, random_state=42)</li>\n                        <li>B) model.feature_importances_()</li>\n                        <li>C) sklearn.metrics.importance_score()</li>\n                        <li>D) model.shuffle_weights()</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    <details>\n                        <summary>Show Answer &amp; Feedback</summary>\n                        <div class="quiz-feedback">\n                            <p><strong>Correct Answer: A</strong> &mdash; <em>permutation_importance(model, X_val, y_val, n_repeats=10) computed empirical performance drops on validation data.</em></p>\n                            <p><strong>Detailed Activity Feedback:</strong> `permutation_importance` shuffles each feature column `n_repeats` times on validation data, measuring the drop in model scoring metric to deliver unbiased feature relevance rankings.</p>\n                            <ul>\n                                <li><strong>Why B is incorrect:</strong> `feature_importances_` is an attribute (not a function) and computes biased MDI on training data.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why C is incorrect:</strong> importance_score is not a valid Scikit-Learn metric function.</li>\n                                <li><strong>Why D is incorrect:</strong> shuffle_weights is a non-existent method.</li>\n                            </ul>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n'}}
